# Line endings.
#
# Every text file is LF in the repository *and* in the working tree, on every
# platform. `eol=lf` is the part that matters: without it, git on Windows
# checks files out as CRLF, and the recorded example transcripts in
# `tests/expected/` then differ from what the interpreter produces, which is
# `\n`. That failed on the Windows CI runners while passing everywhere else.
#
# This also keeps `tools/gen-docs.ts --check` honest: a generator that writes
# LF must not be compared against a working tree that has been rewritten to
# CRLF behind its back.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, so git never inspects or rewrites the contents.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff2 binary

# Generated, and not worth reviewing line by line in a diff.
package-lock.json linguist-generated=true
tests/conformance/*.json linguist-generated=true
docs/stdlib.md linguist-generated=true
docs/errors.md linguist-generated=true

# Baa is the point of the repository, so this asks for it to count towards the
# language statistics rather than being written off as unrecognised text.
#
# It does nothing yet, and that is worth knowing rather than rediscovering:
# `linguist-language` only accepts a name that already exists in Linguist's
# `languages.yml`, and Baa is not in it. Unknown values are ignored, so the
# `.baa` files here are counted as nothing and the bar on GitHub shows only the
# languages the *implementation* is written in.
#
# Getting into Linguist needs real-world use across many repositories, which is
# a consequence of adoption rather than something a repository can arrange. The
# line stays because it becomes correct on the day that happens, and costs
# nothing until then.
*.baa linguist-language=Baa
